# Power Manager Fast FSM Behavioral Specification

## 1. Overview

The fast power-management FSM is responsible for sequencing the system through power-up, low-power entry, low-power exit, and reset handling after the slow power-management controller has made the normal-speed clock available.

The module operates as a finite-state machine that reacts to requests and status indications from other power-management functions. It must never enter or remain in a state that leaves the system without a valid reset or clock configuration, except as part of the defined low-power or reset sequences.

## 2. Reset and Initial State

At reset, the module shall:

- enter the low-power resting state;
- assert the low-power status indication;
- keep all reset request outputs asserted;
- keep all normal IP clock enable requests deasserted;
- deassert the power-up acknowledgment;
- deassert the power-down request;
- disable processing-element fetch;
- deassert wake, fall-through, abort, and clear indications;
- clear the strap-sampled state so that straps will be sampled again on the next power-up sequence;
- remain in this state until a power-up request is received or an internal reset-recovery condition is present.

If the module is in the low-power resting state and no power-up request is present, it must remain there with clocks disabled, resets asserted as appropriate, and fetch disabled.

## 3. Power-Up Sequence

The power-up sequence begins from the low-power resting state when a power-up request is observed. It also begins automatically if the module is recovering from an internally requested reset.

### 3.1 Clock Enable Phase

The module must request that all relevant IP clocks be enabled. It must wait until the clock status inputs indicate that all clocks it has requested to enable are actually enabled before proceeding.

Clocks that are not currently requested to be enabled shall not be required to report an enabled status.

### 3.2 Life-Cycle Reset Release

After clocks are enabled, the module must release the life-cycle reset stage for all power domains. It must wait until reset status inputs indicate that the life-cycle reset sources have actually been released.

During this phase, the system reset stage shall remain asserted.

### 3.3 OTP Initialization

After life-cycle resets are released, the module must drive the OTP initialization request active and wait for OTP initialization completion.

Once OTP initialization completes, the module must deassert the OTP initialization request and proceed to life-cycle initialization.

### 3.4 Life-Cycle Initialization

The module must drive the life-cycle initialization request active and wait for the life-cycle controller initialization completion indication.

Once life-cycle initialization completes, the module proceeds to the power-up acknowledgment phase.

### 3.5 Power-Up Acknowledgment

During a normal power-up, the module must assert the power-up acknowledgment output. It must keep this output asserted until the power-up request is withdrawn. After the request is withdrawn, the module must:

- deassert the power-up acknowledgment;
- assert the clear-configuration-lock indication;
- determine whether a wake interrupt is required;
- clear the internal reset-recovery condition;
- proceed to the ROM integrity check phase.

If the current power-up is part of an internal reset recovery, the module shall not assert the power-up acknowledgment. In that case, the module proceeds directly to the ROM integrity check phase and still asserts the clear-configuration-lock indication when leaving this phase.

### 3.6 ROM Integrity Check

In the ROM integrity check phase, the module must:

- deassert the low-power status indication;
- deassert the system reset requests;
- set the reset cause to no reset;
- wait for the ROM integrity check to pass.

The ROM integrity check is considered successful when the ROM controller reports that its check is done. If the life-cycle state indicates that both DFT and hardware debug enable are active, the integrity-check-good result is not required; otherwise, the ROM controller must report both done and good.

### 3.7 Strap Sampling

After the ROM integrity check passes, the module must perform strap sampling.

The strap output shall be asserted during the strap sampling phase only if strap sampling has not already been completed in this reset/power-up cycle. When asserted, the module records that straps have been sampled and then proceeds to the active state.

The strap-sampling state must be re-armed at reset and whenever all reset requests are asserted, so that the next power-up sequence samples straps again.

### 3.8 Active State

After strap sampling, the module enters the active state. In the active state, the module must:

- enable processing-element fetch;
- keep the low-power status deasserted;
- keep reset cause at no reset;
- keep reset requests deasserted for normally powered domains;
- keep clocks enabled;
- wait for either a low-power entry request or a reset request.

## 4. Low-Power Entry

### 4.1 Detecting a Low-Power Entry Request

A low-power entry request indicates that software has requested low power. When this request is observed while the module is active, the module must immediately disable processing-element fetch and begin the clock-disable sequence.

If a reset request is also asserted at this time, the reset request shall take precedence if it is still present when the clock-disable phase completes.

### 4.2 Clock Disable Phase

During the clock-disable phase, the module must deassert all normal IP clock enable requests. It must wait until clock status inputs indicate that the clocks it has requested to disable are actually disabled.

Once clocks are disabled:

- if a reset request is present, the module shall enter the reset path;
- otherwise, the module shall enter the low-power fall-through check.

Some urgent reset requests, such as alert escalation or main-power instability, may cause the module to bypass the clock-disable wait and proceed directly to the reset path.

The low-power status output must be asserted when the pending event is a low-power entry. It must not be asserted when the pending event is a reset.

### 4.3 Fall-Through Check

During the fall-through check, the module must assert the clear-low-power-hint indication.

If the low-power entry request has already deasserted, the module must:

- re-enable clocks;
- assert the wake interrupt output;
- assert the fall-through indication;
- return to the ROM integrity check phase.

If the low-power entry request is still asserted, the module proceeds to the non-volatile idle check.

### 4.4 Non-Volatile Idle Check

Before entering low power, the module must verify that OTP, the life-cycle controller, and flash are all idle.

If all are idle, the module proceeds to low-power preparation.

If any of them is busy, the module must:

- re-enable clocks;
- assert the wake interrupt output;
- assert the abort indication;
- return to the ROM integrity check phase.

### 4.5 Low-Power Preparation

In the low-power preparation phase, the module must:

- set the reset cause to low-power entry;
- assert reset requests for non-always-on power domains if the main power domain is off;
- leave non-always-on reset requests deasserted if the main power domain remains on;
- wait until reset status is consistent with the requested low-power configuration.

If the main power domain remains on, no reset status wait is required. If the main power domain is off, the module must wait until all affected non-always-on domains report their reset sources asserted.

### 4.6 Power-Down Request

After low-power preparation is complete, the module must:

- assert the power-down request output;
- wait for the power-down acknowledgment;
- deassert the power-down request;
- enter the low-power resting state.

In the low-power resting state, the module keeps the low-power status asserted, clocks disabled, fetch disabled, and reset requests asserted as required by the low-power configuration.

### 4.7 Low-Power Exit

The module exits the low-power resting state only when a new power-up request is received, or when an internal reset-recovery condition is present.

If the power-up cause is a wake event and the previous reset cause was low-power entry, the module must assert the wake interrupt output during the power-up acknowledgment phase.

If the power-up is caused by a reset event, no wake interrupt shall be generated for that power-up.

## 5. Reset Handling

### 5.1 Reset Request While Active

When a reset request is observed while the module is active, the module must disable processing-element fetch and begin the clock-disable sequence.

The reset path is selected when a reset request is still present after clocks are disabled, or when an urgent reset request bypasses the clock-disable wait.

### 5.2 Reset Shutdown

When the reset path is selected, the module must:

- assert the clear-low-power-hint indication;
- set the internal reset-recovery condition;
- proceed to reset preparation.

### 5.3 Reset Preparation

During reset preparation, the module must:

- set the reset cause to hardware-request reset;
- assert all life-cycle and system reset requests for all power domains;
- assert the clear-slow-request output;
- wait until all reset status inputs indicate that all resets are asserted;
- wait until the clear-slow-request output is acknowledged.

When reset preparation completes, the module enters the low-power resting state. Because the internal reset-recovery condition is set, the module immediately starts another power-up sequence without requiring an external power-up request.

### 5.4 Reset During Low Power

A reset event that occurs while the module is already in the low-power resting state is handled by the normal power-up sequence. The module does not need to observe the reset request directly; it responds to the resulting power-up request.

## 6. Invalid State Handling

If the module receives an invalid-state indication from the companion power-management FSM, it must immediately override all normal transitions and enter the terminal invalid state.

In the terminal invalid state, the module must:

- assert all life-cycle and system reset requests;
- deassert all normal IP clock enable requests;
- remain in this state until reset.

## 7. Output and Indication Requirements

The module shall provide the following externally observable behavior:

### 7.1 Power-Up Acknowledgment

The power-up acknowledgment output shall be asserted only during a normal power-up completion handshake. It shall be deasserted during reset recovery and while in low power.

### 7.2 Power-Down Request

The power-down request output shall be asserted only after all low-power preparation conditions are satisfied. It shall be deasserted when the power-down acknowledgment is received.

### 7.3 Low-Power Status

The low-power status output shall be:

- asserted in the low-power resting state;
- asserted during low-power entry once the low-power path is selected;
- asserted during the subsequent power-up sequence until the ROM integrity check phase is reached;
- deasserted during normal active operation;
- deasserted during reset handling when the pending event is a reset.

### 7.4 Wake Interrupt

The wake interrupt output shall be asserted when:

- the module exits low power due to a wake event and the previous reset cause is low-power entry;
- a low-power entry falls through because the entry condition has disappeared;
- a low-power entry is aborted because a non-volatile controller is busy.

### 7.5 Fall-Through and Abort Indications

The fall-through indication shall be asserted only when the low-power entry request disappears during the fall-through check.

The abort indication shall be asserted only when the non-volatile idle check fails.

### 7.6 Clear Indications

The module shall assert a clear-low-power-hint indication during fall-through handling and during reset shutdown. This ensures any pending software low-power entry request is cleared.

The module shall assert a clear-configuration-lock indication when the power-up acknowledgment phase completes.

### 7.7 Reset Requests and Reset Cause

The module shall output reset requests for life-cycle and system reset stages. These requests shall be:

- asserted at reset and in the terminal invalid state;
- asserted for all domains during reset handling;
- asserted for non-always-on domains during low-power entry only when the main power domain is off;
- released in the proper order during power-up.

The reset cause output shall indicate:

- no reset after the ROM integrity check phase and during normal active operation;
- low-power entry when the module is committing to low-power entry;
- hardware-request reset when the module is committing to reset handling;
- an indeterminate or pending value during transitional phases where the cause has not yet been resolved.

### 7.8 Clock Enable Requests

The module shall control normal IP clock enable requests as follows:

- main and IO clock enable requests are controlled together;
- the USB clock enable request is asserted only when the normal clock-enable phase is active and the separate USB enable input is asserted;
- it shall report the USB clock status based on the clock status input.

The module shall not proceed past a clock-enable or clock-disable phase until the relevant clock statuses are consistent with the request.

### 7.9 OTP and Life-Cycle Initialization Requests

The module shall assert the OTP initialization request during the OTP initialization phase and hold it until OTP initialization completes.

The module shall assert the life-cycle initialization request during the life-cycle initialization phase and hold it until life-cycle initialization completes.

### 7.10 Strap Output

The strap output shall be asserted during strap sampling, provided straps have not already been sampled. After the strap output is asserted, the module records that straps have been sampled and deasserts the output.

### 7.11 Fetch Enable

The fetch enable output shall be:

- disabled at reset;
- disabled in all low-power and reset phases;
- disabled immediately when a low-power entry request or reset request is observed while active;
- enabled only in the active state.
